---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';

// Section 3.1.4 of the thesis: against whom the guarantees hold.
const adversaries = [
  {
    who: 'Network, no keys',
    can: 'Replays public certificates and opens connections.',
    gets: 'Computation spent at the receiver.',
    not: 'Admission, because the proof of possession fails.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Holder of a valid certificate',
    can: 'Presents it with whatever chain it likes.',
    gets: 'Its own admission, if it is admissible.',
    not: 'Choosing the path, or widening its names or policies.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Malicious or compromised member domain',
    can: 'Issues certificates, cross-certificates, evidence and statements, and publishes them.',
    gets: 'Admission of its subjects within what others’ constraints leave open; attracting the selection with a cheap path; denials or a longer search in the receivers that reach its keys.',
    not: 'Fabricating others’ edges, escaping a constraint written in a cross-certificate issued to it, or influencing a receiver that does not reach its keys.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Operator of the common log',
    can: 'Omits, delays or reorders entries, shows split views, serves an old state.',
    gets: 'Denials and, while earlier evidence is still valid, acceptance of a certificate already revoked.',
    not: 'Forging entries.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Dishonest witnesses, up to the tolerated number',
    can: 'Cosign any checkpoint.',
    gets: 'Nothing against a reader that demands the minimum quorum.',
    not: 'Making two readers accept incompatible histories.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Cryptanalytic',
    can: 'Signs as any key of a broken algorithm, or as a weak key.',
    gets: 'Admission through any broken node the receiver admits; forged evidence or checkpoints signed with that algorithm.',
    not: 'Anything through the nodes that the floor, the algorithm lists or the exclusions leave out.',
  },
  {
    who: 'Insider of the receiver',
    can: 'Changes the policy, the anchors or the decision record.',
    gets: 'Everything.',
    not: 'Out of scope.',
  },
];

const hypotheses = [
  'The signature algorithms the receiver admits under the last floor of its list resist forgery.',
  'SHA-256 resists collisions.',
  'The clocks of the domains agree within the tolerance the compilation profile fixes.',
  'The dishonest witnesses of the log do not exceed the number each reader tolerates.',
  'The founding charter of the federation reaches each reader through an authentic channel.',
  'The material is published in full, a hypothesis needed only for the negative conclusions of Govern.',
];
---

<Chapter id="threats" num="6" kicker="Threat model" title="Against whom the guarantees hold">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The properties of the model do not hold equally against every adversary, and the assets they protect are four: the integrity of the admission decision and its availability, the confidentiality of each domain’s policy and topology, and the integrity of the record of its decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each domain trusts only what it operates: its governance plane with the compiler, its policy
      and anchors, the validation library with the policy checker, its TLS stack and its clock. The
      common log and the material of the other domains are untrusted inputs, whose content has to
      authenticate itself.
      <span class="sn"
        ><b>Quorum</b> With <span class="m">n</span> witnesses of which at most <span class="m">f</span> are dishonest, a reader that demands <span class="m">q</span>{' '}
        cosignatures needs <span class="m">2q − n &gt; f</span> for two accepted checkpoints to share an honest witness,
        and <span class="m">q ≤ n − f</span> for the honest ones to reach it alone.</span
      >
    </p>
    <p>The guarantees rest on six hypotheses, of which the first five are needed for any decision:</p>
    <ol class="hyp">
      {hypotheses.map((h) => <li>{h}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
    <table class="tbl">
      <caption><b>Table 5.</b> Adversaries considered and the reach of each one.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Adversary</th>
          <th scope="col">Capability</th>
          <th scope="col">Achieves</th>
          <th scope="col">Does not achieve</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          adversaries.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{a.who}</th>
              <td data-label="Capability">{a.can}</td>
              <td data-label="Achieves">{a.gets}</td>
              <td data-label="Does not achieve">{a.not}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The guarantee is strong against the network adversary, the certificate holder and the witnesses, whereas against a member domain or the log operator the design limits the damage without
      reducing it to a mere denial, and the case that prevents it is the omission of evidence, because whoever
      withholds the latest revocation list leaves the previous one in force for as long as the
      receiver’s freshness bound <span class="m">F<sub>d</sub></span> allows. Against the cryptanalytic adversary the floor reaches the keys that sign the evidence, because in the direct profile each list is signed by a key of the path itself, but it does not cover
      the signature of the log or the authentication of the channel. Finally, a domain that the receiver does not reach from its anchors cannot move any of its decisions, which is the confinement of section 5.8.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .hyp {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    list-style: decimal;
  }

  .hyp li {
    padding: 0.15rem 0 0.15rem 0.3rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .hyp li::marker {
    color: var(--brass);
  }

  .wide {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .wide th[scope='row'] {
    width: 22%;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .wide th[scope='row'] {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }


</style>
